// Spliced into the deployed ruleset by scripts/deploy-chat-presence-rules.mjs,
// which also removes whatever chat_presence block is already live. It has to
// remove it: Firestore ORs every matching rule together, so a second, stricter
// match block next to `allow read, write: if true` would change nothing at all.
//
// What each client actually needs here, and nothing else:
//   list    — the count() aggregation both widgets poll for the viewer number.
//             Aggregations authorise as queries, so this is `list`, not `get`.
//   create  — a device's first heartbeat.
//   update  — every heartbeat after that, once a minute.
//
// Nothing reads an individual row, and no browser deletes anything any more —
// scripts/purge-stale-presence.mjs does that on a schedule through the Admin
// SDK, which bypasses these rules entirely.
//
// The write shape is pinned to exactly one numeric field so this collection
// can't be used as free arbitrary storage, and lastSeen is held near real
// time so nobody can park a row far in the future and inflate the viewer
// count permanently. The 5-minute slack is for genuinely skewed device
// clocks: lastSeen is Date.now() from the browser, not a server timestamp,
// because the count query has to filter on a plain number.
match /chat_presence/{id} {
  allow get: if false;
  allow list: if true;
  allow create, update: if request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['lastSeen'])
    && request.resource.data.lastSeen is number
    && request.resource.data.lastSeen <= request.time.toMillis() + 300000
    && request.resource.data.lastSeen >= request.time.toMillis() - 300000;
  allow delete: if false;
}
